const e="Supporting a Partner Through Eating Disorder Treatment in Brisbane | Michelle Barratt Psychology",a="Standing Beside Your Partner Through Recovery Supporting a partner through eating disorder treatment in Brisbane can feel confusing and heavy. You might be wo",t='<!--$--><section class="bg-gradient-to-br from-plum to-rose text-white"><div class="mx-auto max-w-7xl px-5 py-14 md:py-20"><div><nav aria-label="Breadcrumb" class="mb-6"><ol class="flex flex-wrap items-center gap-2 text-[12px] uppercase tracking-[0.14em] text-white/75"><li><a class="hover:text-white" href="/">Home</a></li><li aria-hidden="true"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="24" height="24" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" class="lucide lucide-chevron-right h-3 w-3"><path d="m9 18 6-6-6-6"></path></svg></li><li><a class="hover:text-white active" href="/blog" data-status="active" aria-current="page">Blog</a></li><li aria-hidden="true"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="24" height="24" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" class="lucide lucide-chevron-right h-3 w-3"><path d="m9 18 6-6-6-6"></path></svg></li><li class="text-white" aria-current="page">Supporting a Partner Through Eating Disorder Treatment in Brisbane</li></ol></nav><p class="text-[12px] font-semibold uppercase tracking-[0.2em] text-white/85 mb-3">29 May 2026</p><h1 class="text-white text-[32px] md:text-[44px] font-semibold leading-[1.08] max-w-3xl">Supporting a Partner Through Eating Disorder Treatment in Brisbane</h1><div class="h-[3px] w-16 bg-sun my-5" aria-hidden="true"></div><div class="max-w-2xl text-[16px] md:text-[17px] text-white/95 space-y-3"><p>Standing Beside Your Partner Through Recovery Supporting a partner through eating disorder treatment in Brisbane can feel confusing and heavy. You might be worried about saying the wrong thing, scared about their health, and unsure how to h</p></div><div class="mt-6 flex flex-wrap gap-3"><a class="inline-flex items-center justify-center min-h-12 px-7 rounded whitespace-nowrap text-[13px] font-semibold uppercase tracking-[0.12em] transition-colors duration-300 bg-white text-plum hover:bg-blush " href="/contact">Contact Us</a></div></div></div></section><section class="bg-soft !py-0"><div class="mx-auto max-w-7xl px-5 py-14 md:py-20 "><div class="py-14 md:py-16"><img src="https://michellebarrattpsychology.com.au/wp-content/uploads/2026/05/eating-disorder.jpg" alt="" class="w-full aspect-[16/9] max-h-[520px] object-cover rounded border border-lilac"/></div></div></section><section class="bg-white "><div class="mx-auto max-w-7xl px-5 py-14 md:py-20 "><div class="mb-10 max-w-3xl"><h2 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-semibold">Standing Beside Your Partner Through Recovery</h2><div class="h-[3px] w-14 bg-sun mt-5" aria-hidden="true"></div></div><p class="max-w-3xl text-[17px] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90 mb-5">Supporting a partner through eating disorder treatment in Brisbane can feel confusing and heavy. You might be worried about saying the wrong thing, scared about their health, and unsure how to help without making things worse. Your care is important, and with the right guidance, you can be a steady, kind presence in their recovery.</p><p class="max-w-3xl text-[17px] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90 mb-5">Eating disorders affect both the person and the relationship. You may see emotional distance, secrecy around food, changes in mood, or withdrawal from social life. This is not about you being a bad partner, and it is not your job to fix the eating disorder. Professional help, like clinical psychology support here in Brisbane, gives structure and safety so both of you feel less alone.</p><p class="max-w-3xl text-[17px] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90 mb-5">Our main message is simple: you cannot cure the eating disorder, but you can be one of the strongest supports in your partner’s treatment and recovery. Your consistency, patience, and willingness to learn can make day-to-day life feel safer for both of you.</p></div></section><section class="bg-soft "><div class="mx-auto max-w-7xl px-5 py-14 md:py-20 "><div class="mb-10 max-w-3xl"><h2 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-semibold">Understanding Eating Disorders and Treatment in Brisbane</h2><div class="h-[3px] w-14 bg-sun mt-5" aria-hidden="true"></div></div><p class="max-w-3xl text-[17px] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90 mb-5">Eating disorders are serious mental health conditions, not phases, diets gone too far, or lifestyle choices. Common types include:</p><ul class="max-w-3xl space-y-2.5 my-6"><li class="flex gap-3 text-[17px] leading-relaxed text-ink/90"><span class="mt-[10px] h-1.5 w-1.5 shrink-0 rounded-full bg-rose" aria-hidden="true"></span><span>Anorexia nervosa, where there is intense fear of weight gain and often significant food restriction</span></li><li class="flex gap-3 text-[17px] leading-relaxed text-ink/90"><span class="mt-[10px] h-1.5 w-1.5 shrink-0 rounded-full bg-rose" aria-hidden="true"></span><span>Bulimia nervosa, which involves cycles of binge eating and compensatory behaviours like vomiting or extreme exercise</span></li><li class="flex gap-3 text-[17px] leading-relaxed text-ink/90"><span class="mt-[10px] h-1.5 w-1.5 shrink-0 rounded-full bg-rose" aria-hidden="true"></span><span>Binge eating disorder, with episodes of eating large amounts of food while feeling out of control and very distressed</span></li><li class="flex gap-3 text-[17px] leading-relaxed text-ink/90"><span class="mt-[10px] h-1.5 w-1.5 shrink-0 rounded-full bg-rose" aria-hidden="true"></span><span>ARFID (Avoidant/Restrictive Food Intake Disorder), where someone has very limited intake, often related to fear of consequences like choking, or strong sensory reactions to food</span></li></ul><p class="max-w-3xl text-[17px] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90 mb-5">These conditions affect thoughts, feelings, behaviour, and the body. They can lead to serious physical and emotional risks, so early, specialised treatment really matters.</p><p class="max-w-3xl text-[17px] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90 mb-5">In Brisbane, evidence-based eating disorder treatment usually includes a mix of supports, such as:</p><ul class="max-w-3xl space-y-2.5 my-6"><li class="flex gap-3 text-[17px] leading-relaxed text-ink/90"><span class="mt-[10px] h-1.5 w-1.5 shrink-0 rounded-full bg-rose" aria-hidden="true"></span><span>A thorough clinical assessment with a qualified mental health professional</span></li><li class="flex gap-3 text-[17px] leading-relaxed text-ink/90"><span class="mt-[10px] h-1.5 w-1.5 shrink-0 rounded-full bg-rose" aria-hidden="true"></span><span>Individual therapy that targets thoughts, emotions, and behaviours around food and body image</span></li><li class="flex gap-3 text-[17px] leading-relaxed text-ink/90"><span class="mt-[10px] h-1.5 w-1.5 shrink-0 rounded-full bg-rose" aria-hidden="true"></span><span>Involvement of family or partners, especially when home support is a key part of treatment</span></li><li class="flex gap-3 text-[17px] leading-relaxed text-ink/90"><span class="mt-[10px] h-1.5 w-1.5 shrink-0 rounded-full bg-rose" aria-hidden="true"></span><span>Regular medical checks with a GP or paediatrician to monitor physical health</span></li><li class="flex gap-3 text-[17px] leading-relaxed text-ink/90"><span class="mt-[10px] h-1.5 w-1.5 shrink-0 rounded-full bg-rose" aria-hidden="true"></span><span>Input from a dietitian who understands eating disorders</span></li></ul><p class="max-w-3xl text-[17px] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90 mb-5">At a Brisbane-based practice like ours, psychologists often work closely with GPs, paediatricians, psychiatrists, and dietitians. The aim is to build a consistent team that understands your partner’s goals and keeps everyone on the same page, including you as their support person.</p></div></section><section class="bg-white "><div class="mx-auto max-w-7xl px-5 py-14 md:py-20 "><div class="mb-10 max-w-3xl"><h2 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-semibold">Practical Ways to Support Your Partner Day to Day</h2><div class="h-[3px] w-14 bg-sun mt-5" aria-hidden="true"></div></div><p class="max-w-3xl text-[17px] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90 mb-5">Daily life can feel like the hardest part of eating disorder treatment in Brisbane, because so many triggers show up around food, body image, and social situations. Small, steady actions from you can help.</p><p class="max-w-3xl text-[17px] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90 mb-5">Supportive behaviours at home might include:</p><ul class="max-w-3xl space-y-2.5 my-6"><li class="flex gap-3 text-[17px] leading-relaxed text-ink/90"><span class="mt-[10px] h-1.5 w-1.5 shrink-0 rounded-full bg-rose" aria-hidden="true"></span><span>Validating their feelings, for example, “I can see this is really hard, and I’m here with you”</span></li><li class="flex gap-3 text-[17px] leading-relaxed text-ink/90"><span class="mt-[10px] h-1.5 w-1.5 shrink-0 rounded-full bg-rose" aria-hidden="true"></span><span>Respecting agreed treatment plans from the care team</span></li><li class="flex gap-3 text-[17px] leading-relaxed text-ink/90"><span class="mt-[10px] h-1.5 w-1.5 shrink-0 rounded-full bg-rose" aria-hidden="true"></span><span>Helping keep routines flexible around appointments or therapy homework</span></li><li class="flex gap-3 text-[17px] leading-relaxed text-ink/90"><span class="mt-[10px] h-1.5 w-1.5 shrink-0 rounded-full bg-rose" aria-hidden="true"></span><span>Offering to sit with them before and after meals if that fits with the treatment plan</span></li></ul><p class="max-w-3xl text-[17px] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90 mb-5">Around food and body image, it is usually helpful to:</p><ul class="max-w-3xl space-y-2.5 my-6"><li class="flex gap-3 text-[17px] leading-relaxed text-ink/90"><span class="mt-[10px] h-1.5 w-1.5 shrink-0 rounded-full bg-rose" aria-hidden="true"></span><span>Avoid diet talk, weight loss chat, or comments about “good” and “bad” foods</span></li><li class="flex gap-3 text-[17px] leading-relaxed text-ink/90"><span class="mt-[10px] h-1.5 w-1.5 shrink-0 rounded-full bg-rose" aria-hidden="true"></span><span>Not comment on their body, weight, or shape, even in a “positive” way</span></li><li class="flex gap-3 text-[17px] leading-relaxed text-ink/90"><span class="mt-[10px] h-1.5 w-1.5 shrink-0 rounded-full bg-rose" aria-hidden="true"></span><span>Follow the guidance of your partner and their treatment team about meal support</span></li><li class="flex gap-3 text-[17px] leading-relaxed text-ink/90"><span class="mt-[10px] h-1.5 w-1.5 shrink-0 rounded-full bg-rose" aria-hidden="true"></span><span>Steer conversations toward feelings, values, and life outside the eating disorder</span></li></ul><p class="max-w-3xl text-[17px] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90 mb-5">Brisbane’s late autumn and early winter can bring cozy meals, comfort foods, and more indoor time. Cooler nights might mean more social events built around food, like dinners or shared takeaway. For someone in treatment, these changes can be triggering.</p><p class="max-w-3xl text-[17px] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90 mb-5">You can gently plan ahead together by:</p><ul class="max-w-3xl space-y-2.5 my-6"><li class="flex gap-3 text-[17px] leading-relaxed text-ink/90"><span class="mt-[10px] h-1.5 w-1.5 shrink-0 rounded-full bg-rose" aria-hidden="true"></span><span>Looking at upcoming events and deciding what feels manageable</span></li><li class="flex gap-3 text-[17px] leading-relaxed text-ink/90"><span class="mt-[10px] h-1.5 w-1.5 shrink-0 rounded-full bg-rose" aria-hidden="true"></span><span>Agreeing on an “exit plan” if things feel too hard, like a phrase you use to leave early</span></li><li class="flex gap-3 text-[17px] leading-relaxed text-ink/90"><span class="mt-[10px] h-1.5 w-1.5 shrink-0 rounded-full bg-rose" aria-hidden="true"></span><span>Choosing some non-food activities, such as a walk by the river, a movie night at home, or a craft or hobby you both enjoy</span></li></ul></div></section><section class="bg-soft "><div class="mx-auto max-w-7xl px-5 py-14 md:py-20 "><div class="mb-10 max-w-3xl"><h2 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-semibold">Communicating with Care During Treatment</h2><div class="h-[3px] w-14 bg-sun mt-5" aria-hidden="true"></div></div><p class="max-w-3xl text-[17px] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90 mb-5">How you speak with your partner can make a big difference to how safe they feel during eating disorder treatment in Brisbane. The goal is to be kind and clear, without blame.</p><p class="max-w-3xl text-[17px] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90 mb-5">Some helpful communication tools are:</p><ul class="max-w-3xl space-y-2.5 my-6"><li class="flex gap-3 text-[17px] leading-relaxed text-ink/90"><span class="mt-[10px] h-1.5 w-1.5 shrink-0 rounded-full bg-rose" aria-hidden="true"></span><span>Use “I” statements, such as “I feel worried when…” instead of “You always…”</span></li><li class="flex gap-3 text-[17px] leading-relaxed text-ink/90"><span class="mt-[10px] h-1.5 w-1.5 shrink-0 rounded-full bg-rose" aria-hidden="true"></span><span>Listen more than you speak, and try to reflect back what you hear</span></li><li class="flex gap-3 text-[17px] leading-relaxed text-ink/90"><span class="mt-[10px] h-1.5 w-1.5 shrink-0 rounded-full bg-rose" aria-hidden="true"></span><span>Choose calmer, private moments for harder conversations, not right in the middle of a crisis</span></li></ul><p class="max-w-3xl text-[17px] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90 mb-5">When your partner is distressed before or after appointments, meals, or social events, try to:</p><ul class="max-w-3xl space-y-2.5 my-6"><li class="flex gap-3 text-[17px] leading-relaxed text-ink/90"><span class="mt-[10px] h-1.5 w-1.5 shrink-0 rounded-full bg-rose" aria-hidden="true"></span><span>Stay physically present, even if you do not know what to say</span></li><li class="flex gap-3 text-[17px] leading-relaxed text-ink/90"><span class="mt-[10px] h-1.5 w-1.5 shrink-0 rounded-full bg-rose" aria-hidden="true"></span><span>Ask what they need in that moment, for example, “Do you want a hug, some space, or for me to just sit with you?”</span></li><li class="flex gap-3 text-[17px] leading-relaxed text-ink/90"><span class="mt-[10px] h-1.5 w-1.5 shrink-0 rounded-full bg-rose" aria-hidden="true"></span><span>Gently suggest simple grounding strategies, like noticing five things they can see, four things they can feel, or taking some slow breaths together</span></li><li class="flex gap-3 text-[17px] leading-relaxed text-ink/90"><span class="mt-[10px] h-1.5 w-1.5 shrink-0 rounded-full bg-rose" aria-hidden="true"></span><span>Respect their limits, while still letting them know you care and are not going anywhere</span></li></ul><p class="max-w-3xl text-[17px] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90 mb-5">If you are unsure about how to respond to certain behaviours or comments, it is okay to bring your questions to the treatment team. Many Brisbane clinicians welcome partner involvement. You might ask to attend part of an appointment or to have a separate session focused on how you can best support your partner, so your efforts line up with the overall treatment plan.</p></div></section><section class="bg-white "><div class="mx-auto max-w-7xl px-5 py-14 md:py-20 "><div class="mb-10 max-w-3xl"><h2 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-semibold">Looking After Yourself While You Support Them</h2><div class="h-[3px] w-14 bg-sun mt-5" aria-hidden="true"></div></div><p class="max-w-3xl text-[17px] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90 mb-5">Your wellbeing is part of the recovery environment. When you are exhausted, resentful, or anxious all the time, it can add stress for both of you. Taking care of yourself is not selfish; it is part of caring for your partner.</p><p class="max-w-3xl text-[17px] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90 mb-5">Self-care does not have to be big or fancy. In everyday Brisbane life, it might look like:</p><ul class="max-w-3xl space-y-2.5 my-6"><li class="flex gap-3 text-[17px] leading-relaxed text-ink/90"><span class="mt-[10px] h-1.5 w-1.5 shrink-0 rounded-full bg-rose" aria-hidden="true"></span><span>Keeping up one or two of your own hobbies or interests</span></li><li class="flex gap-3 text-[17px] leading-relaxed text-ink/90"><span class="mt-[10px] h-1.5 w-1.5 shrink-0 rounded-full bg-rose" aria-hidden="true"></span><span>Having regular movement that feels good for you, like walking, swimming, or yoga</span></li><li class="flex gap-3 text-[17px] leading-relaxed text-ink/90"><span class="mt-[10px] h-1.5 w-1.5 shrink-0 rounded-full bg-rose" aria-hidden="true"></span><span>Making time for rest, including sleep and quiet moments on your own</span></li><li class="flex gap-3 text-[17px] leading-relaxed text-ink/90"><span class="mt-[10px] h-1.5 w-1.5 shrink-0 rounded-full bg-rose" aria-hidden="true"></span><span>Staying connected to trusted friends or family members who can listen without judgement</span></li></ul><p class="max-w-3xl text-[17px] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90 mb-5">It can also help to have your own professional support. Speaking with a psychologist who understands eating disorders and relationships gives you a space to share your worries, learn skills, and feel less alone. At Michelle Barratt Psychology, we involve partners and families in a structured way where this fits with the person’s treatment plan, so the relationship gets support too.</p></div></section><section class="bg-soft "><div class="mx-auto max-w-7xl px-5 py-14 md:py-20 "><div class="mb-10 max-w-3xl"><h2 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-semibold">Taking the Next Step Together in Brisbane</h2><div class="h-[3px] w-14 bg-sun mt-5" aria-hidden="true"></div></div><p class="max-w-3xl text-[17px] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90 mb-5">Seeking specialised eating disorder treatment in Brisbane is an act of care for both your partner and your relationship. Accepting help does not mean you have failed as a couple; it means you are choosing safety, support, and a path forward.</p><p class="max-w-3xl text-[17px] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90 mb-5">You do not need to change everything at once. You might start with one or two small steps this week, such as sitting in on part of an appointment if that is appropriate, planning one supportive meal together following the treatment team’s guidance, or setting up a simple, non-food-focused activity you can both look forward to.</p><p class="max-w-3xl text-[17px] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90 mb-5">At Michelle Barratt Psychology, we offer compassionate, evidence-based care for children, adolescents, adults, couples and families, including specialised support for eating disorders. Our team works with you and other Brisbane health professionals to build a consistent, caring treatment plan that supports both your partner’s recovery and your relationship.</p></div></section><section class="bg-white "><div class="mx-auto max-w-7xl px-5 py-14 md:py-20 "><div class="mb-10 max-w-3xl"><h2 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-semibold">Take A Confident Step Toward Recovery Today</h2><div class="h-[3px] w-14 bg-sun mt-5" aria-hidden="true"></div></div><p class="max-w-3xl text-[17px] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90 mb-5">If you or someone you care about is struggling with disordered eating, we are here at Michelle Barratt Psychology to support you with evidence-based, compassionate care. Learn more about how our eating disorder treatment in Brisbane can help you work toward a healthier relationship with food, body and self. When you feel ready, please contact us so we can discuss your situation and explore the next best step together.</p></div></section><section class="bg-blush "><div class="mx-auto max-w-7xl px-5 py-14 md:py-20 "><h2 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-semibold mb-8">More articles</h2><div class="grid gap-8 sm:grid-cols-2 lg:grid-cols-3"><a class="group bg-white rounded overflow-hidden border border-lilac flex flex-col transition-shadow duration-300 hover:shadow-lg" href="/blog/recognising-ptsd-counselling-needs"><div class="aspect-[3/2] bg-lilac overflow-hidden"><img src="https://michellebarrattpsychology.com.au/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/recognising-ptsd-counselling-needs-featured.webp" alt="" loading="lazy" class="h-full w-full object-cover transition-transform duration-500 group-hover:scale-105"/></div><div class="p-6 flex flex-col flex-1"><p class="text-[11px] font-semibold uppercase tracking-[0.16em] text-rose mb-2">23 October 2026</p><h3 class="text-xl font-semibold leading-snug">Recognising PTSD Counselling Needs in Toowong Adults</h3><p class="mt-3 text-[15px] leading-relaxed text-ink/80 line-clamp-3">Post-traumatic stress disorder, or PTSD, is not only something that happens to people in movies or in extreme situations. It can grow quietly in everyday lif…</p><span class="mt-4 inline-flex items-center gap-2 text-[13px] font-semibold uppercase tracking-[0.12em] text-ocean">Read article<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="24" height="24" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" class="lucide lucide-arrow-up-right h-4 w-4" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M7 7h10v10"></path><path d="M7 17 17 7"></path></svg></span></div></a><a class="group bg-white rounded overflow-hidden border border-lilac flex flex-col transition-shadow duration-300 hover:shadow-lg" href="/blog/online-therapy-in-brisbane-when-you"><div class="aspect-[3/2] bg-lilac overflow-hidden"><img src="https://michellebarrattpsychology.com.au/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/online-therapy-in-brisbane-when-you-featured.webp" alt="" loading="lazy" class="h-full w-full object-cover transition-transform duration-500 group-hover:scale-105"/></div><div class="p-6 flex flex-col flex-1"><p class="text-[11px] font-semibold uppercase tracking-[0.16em] text-rose mb-2">16 October 2026</p><h3 class="text-xl font-semibold leading-snug">Online Therapy in Brisbane When You Can’t Find Local Support</h3><p class="mt-3 text-[15px] leading-relaxed text-ink/80 line-clamp-3">Trying to find a psychologist in Brisbane can feel exhausting. You search, you join waitlists, you juggle your calendar, and still there is no appointment th…</p><span class="mt-4 inline-flex items-center gap-2 text-[13px] font-semibold uppercase tracking-[0.12em] text-ocean">Read article<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="24" height="24" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" class="lucide lucide-arrow-up-right h-4 w-4" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M7 7h10v10"></path><path d="M7 17 17 7"></path></svg></span></div></a><a class="group bg-white rounded overflow-hidden border border-lilac flex flex-col transition-shadow duration-300 hover:shadow-lg" href="/blog/recognising-trauma-counselling-needs"><div class="aspect-[3/2] bg-lilac overflow-hidden"><img src="https://michellebarrattpsychology.com.au/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/recognising-trauma-counselling-needs-featured.webp" alt="" loading="lazy" class="h-full w-full object-cover transition-transform duration-500 group-hover:scale-105"/></div><div class="p-6 flex flex-col flex-1"><p class="text-[11px] font-semibold uppercase tracking-[0.16em] text-rose mb-2">9 October 2026</p><h3 class="text-xl font-semibold leading-snug">Recognising Trauma Counselling Needs in Toowong Professionals</h3><p class="mt-3 text-[15px] leading-relaxed text-ink/80 line-clamp-3">High-performing professionals in Toowong are often good at holding it together. Work is busy, the pressure is high, and most people tell themselves it is jus…</p><span class="mt-4 inline-flex items-center gap-2 text-[13px] font-semibold uppercase tracking-[0.12em] text-ocean">Read article<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="24" height="24" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" class="lucide lucide-arrow-up-right h-4 w-4" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M7 7h10v10"></path><path d="M7 17 17 7"></path></svg></span></div></a></div></div></section><section class="bg-blush"><div class="mx-auto max-w-4xl px-5 py-14 md:py-16 text-center"><p class="text-[12px] font-semibold uppercase tracking-[0.2em] text-rose mb-2.5">Take the first step</p><h2 class="text-[30px] md:text-[40px] font-semibold leading-[1.1]">Ready to Take the First Step?</h2><div class="h-[3px] w-14 bg-sun mt-4 mx-auto" aria-hidden="true"></div><p class="mt-5 text-[17px]">Seeking support can feel overwhelming, but you do not have to navigate life&#x27;s challenges alone. Our administration team can help answer your questions, explain appointment options and connect you with a suitable practitioner.</p><p class="mt-3">Prefer to call? Phone our administration team on <a class="text-ocean underline underline-offset-4 font-medium" href="tel:0731850040">07 3185 0040</a>.</p><div class="mt-7 flex flex-wrap justify-center gap-3"><a class="inline-flex items-center justify-center min-h-12 px-7 rounded whitespace-nowrap text-[13px] font-semibold uppercase tracking-[0.12em] transition-colors duration-300 bg-rose text-white hover:bg-[#9d2a59] " href="/practitioners">Book Online</a><a class="inline-flex items-center justify-center min-h-12 px-7 rounded whitespace-nowrap text-[13px] font-semibold uppercase tracking-[0.12em] transition-colors duration-300 bg-ocean text-white hover:bg-[#095a6f] " href="/contact">Contact Us</a><a class="inline-flex items-center justify-center min-h-12 px-7 rounded whitespace-nowrap text-[13px] font-semibold uppercase tracking-[0.12em] transition-colors duration-300 border border-plum text-plum hover:bg-lilac " href="/therapy-services">Explore Services</a></div><p class="mt-5 text-sm text-ink/70">If in crisis, please call 000.</p></div></section><!--/$-->',s={title:e,description:a,html:t};export{s as default,a as description,t as html,e as title};
